WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:19.520
MICHAEL HODIN: We 
would like to welcome everyone to today's

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.040
meeting; delighted to have you here.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.470
I'm Mike Hodin, adjunct senior fellow at the
Council on Foreign Relations, running a studies

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:32.990
program on aging populations.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:39.980
Kelly Michel, senior executive at AEGON Corporation,
a global insurance firm.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.370
Many of you here in America know it as Transamerica.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.920
AEGON is a global company based out of The
Hague in The Netherlands.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.800
And Dr. Joe Coughlin, founder and head of
MIT AgeLab.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.469
You have the bios in your materials, so we
won't spend much time on that.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:05.500
Today's meeting is co-sponsored by the Washington
meetings program and the studies program at

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.500
CFR.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.030
It is on the record.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:18.910
But before I go much further, I did want to
give you an explanation of why there are only

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:26.510
three of us on the podium here, and not the
fourth, Undersecretary Bob Hormats.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:31.340
For those of you who did not read your emails
that you got, it was either yesterday -- I

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.180
guess it was yesterday -- which was a reminder
that indicated that at the last minute, unfortunately,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:42.540
the undersecretary, due to another commitment
-- actually, a couple of blocks from here

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.659
-- is unable to join us.

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:48.600
So we do apologize for that.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:55.200
But I can assure you that it was a responsibility,
and we within CFR understand that these things

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:01.659
happen, particularly with someone like the
undersecretary who, obviously, has these senior

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:04.840
political responsibilities.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:13.190
But I did want to open by turning your attention
to a post that the undersecretary did which

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:21.880
appeared yesterday afternoon in the Huffington
Post to, not least, indicate his interest

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:30.730
in this topic and command of its framing,
titled "Aging Population, Economic Growth

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:36.060
and Global Competitiveness," a reference to
this meeting here today -- which, by the way,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.239
is on the record.

00:02:39.239 --> 00:02:46.810
And it's about the U.S. aging population as
an economic growth driver for global competitiveness.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:52.540
And a couple of the references that the undersecretary
makes: It is vital that we create opportunities

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:58.150
to enable older persons to contribute to their
economies and communities in increasingly

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.760
effective and productive ways.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:06.709
This will require new policies and innovations,
and promote healthy aging.

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:12.099
And then he goes on to say: Providing opportunities
for continued contribution by an aging population

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:17.830
is an economic imperative in a growing number
of countries, both developed and developing,

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:25.760
including, of course, the United States.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.420
And then, toward the conclusion: We need a
focused, societywide effort to transform our

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:38.010
vision of aging from a time of dependency
to a time of continued growth, contribution

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.989
and social and economic participation.

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:45.140
So I think that really sets the framework
for us.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:50.190
So let me give you a couple of other points
of introduction, and then we'll immediately

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:56.930
go to our discussion, which we will undertake
for about 20 or 25 minutes among us here,

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:06.540
and then open it up for the second 30 minutes
for audience participation and questions and/or

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:07.930
comments.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:15.650
As indicated, this is a somewhat unusual event
in Council on Foreign Relations framework.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:22.589
It is a collaboration between the studies
program, which has our aging populations framework

00:04:22.589 --> 00:04:27.379
undertaking reports and studies and round
tables and meetings of this kind; but then

00:04:27.379 --> 00:04:41.289
also, CFR broadly has a program celebrating
its 90th anniversary this year, from last

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:44.600
September to September of 2012.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:50.129
And the framework for that is renewing America
and America's strength in the world, America's

00:04:50.129 --> 00:04:52.629
competitiveness.

00:04:52.629 --> 00:04:59.900
Many of us think of that topic as related
to pure economic and fiscal policy, perhaps

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.249
military policy.

00:05:02.249 --> 00:05:08.150
What we're here to discuss today is the relationship
that that has to our aging populations.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:15.479
In this 21st century, our new demographic
reality is such that America and all of our

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:22.279
G-20 partners, and a growing number of countries
around the world, including developing countries,

00:05:22.279 --> 00:05:32.529
align to trends of urbanization, globalization;
have populations where there are more people

00:05:32.529 --> 00:05:38.580
over 50, 55, than under that in working age.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:43.509
This will be a growing trend during the course
of the next several decades.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:49.339
And in a word, the idea that we can remain
competitiveness -- remain competitive, the

00:05:49.339 --> 00:05:56.210
idea that we could maintain fiscal sustainability,
not to speak of looking for economic growth,

00:05:56.210 --> 00:06:03.400
in an environment where this aging population
demographic reality has shifted, is something

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.379
we want to bring to our discussions around
public policy generally, and foreign policy

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:09.919
in particular.

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:16.919
So perhaps we can open up, and I wanted to
just -- I have a few suggested questions that

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:20.240
we might at least start the conversation.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:28.339
Joe, perhaps you could give us an opening
by reviewing some of the data, the demographics

00:06:28.339 --> 00:06:32.900
of where we are in America, where some of
the other countries, our competitors, are,

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.479
and where we stand in that context.

00:06:34.479 --> 00:06:35.479
&gt;&gt;JOSEPH COUGHLIN: Sure.

00:06:35.479 --> 00:06:39.560
You know, it's very interesting that aging
has been quite often -- I would say it has

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.009
been dominantly told as a story of concern.

00:06:43.009 --> 00:06:47.520
When was the last time any of us felt that
living longer was a problem?

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.160
We should be declaring -- giving ourselves
an applause for getting the ability to live

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:54.160
longer.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.099
Essentially, what we're looking at is two
converging forces: a(n) increase in longevity,

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:05.710
where life expectancy at the year 1900 was
somewhere about 45, 47 years old -- 45 probably

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:11.199
in the United States; 47 roughly in Europe
-- to today, if you look at some statistics,

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:12.959
is well into your 70s.

00:07:12.959 --> 00:07:17.439
But interestingly enough, the fastest-growing
part of the population is 85-plus.

00:07:17.439 --> 00:07:19.599
By the way, within that cohort is 100-plus.

00:07:19.599 --> 00:07:21.860
So, yes, we are living longer.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.209
And essentially, if you can get kids to live
past 5 years old, or past 10 years old, you

00:07:26.209 --> 00:07:30.990
get to live probably a pretty good, long,
healthy life in general, or at least manage

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:31.990
well.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.539
That's one part of the story.

00:07:33.539 --> 00:07:37.309
The other part of the story is -- is that,
as income and education goes up, fertility

00:07:37.309 --> 00:07:39.960
tends to go very far down.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:44.520
So we're looking at countries in Europe, for
instance, that have a fertility rate of 1.7,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.229
1.8 children per female.

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:50.529
You need 2.1 just to keep the population even.

00:07:50.529 --> 00:07:53.680
The United States with immigration like -- is
at about 2.1.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:58.419
We are the few (sic), and some would even
argue the only, industrialized country still

00:07:58.419 --> 00:07:59.419
growing.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:01.259
So anecdotally, what do these numbers mean?

00:08:01.259 --> 00:08:06.060
Well, let's -- you can see a lot by looking,
as the great philosopher Yogi Berra said.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.819
There are now more wheelchairs and walkers
in places like Italy, Germany and France,

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:13.399
than there are, say, baby carriages.

00:08:13.399 --> 00:08:16.059
In the United States -- we'll do a test here.

00:08:16.059 --> 00:08:19.969
How many of you were born between 1946 and
'64?

00:08:19.969 --> 00:08:21.300
These are the Baby Boomers.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:24.809
The people who don't have their hands up are
younger, wish you'd just be quiet; and the

00:08:24.809 --> 00:08:27.349
older folks would wish you'd just go away.

00:08:27.349 --> 00:08:28.349
(Laughter.)

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:32.779
One of us is turning 66 -- one every seven
or eight seconds.

00:08:32.779 --> 00:08:34.940
And is this just an industrialized world phenomenon?

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:35.940
Absolutely not.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:40.740
The one child, one family policy of China
has had a demographic destiny of where there's

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.839
165 million people over age 60 there.

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:47.449
And by the way, as we're looking at nations,
as we're looking at regions, we must look

00:08:47.449 --> 00:08:49.660
within nations as well.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:53.959
The average farmer in Indonesia -- one of
the most populous countries in the world,

00:08:53.959 --> 00:08:58.400
and indeed one of the youngest -- but now
the average farmer is 47.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.610
So it's not just a matter of looking at a
region, looking at a nation; we have to look

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.830
at industries, we have to look at specific
regions, and we have to plan to make aging

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:09.199
an opportunity not just to live longer, but
to live better.

00:09:09.199 --> 00:09:10.199
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Thank you.

00:09:10.199 --> 00:09:12.710
Great, great contextual start.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:19.620
So Kelly, as one thinks of this topic of aging
populations, many people automatically go

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:25.329
to the policies and innovations around health.

00:09:25.329 --> 00:09:27.980
And we'll get to that, and that's clear.

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:34.420
But there are other topics as well, and one,
of course, is critical around financial policy,

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.220
financial security.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.220
Perhaps you can give us some sense of where
the innovative thinking in that is.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:41.450
&gt;&gt;KELLY MICHEL: Thank you.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.510
First, let me start off by saying thank you
for having me on the panel.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.329
And I'm honored to be here for the 90th anniversary.

00:09:48.329 --> 00:09:54.079
The center has expand -- or has exceeded the
actuarial tables of most organizations, or

00:09:54.079 --> 00:09:56.290
at least people's life spans -- (laughter)
-- so that's always the good news.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.149
We can all aspire to be 90.

00:09:59.149 --> 00:10:02.149
Particularly relevant to our topic today.

00:10:02.149 --> 00:10:09.410
It is a really exciting time to be in our
business, which is the business of protecting

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:14.009
people's futures with lots of type of financial
instruments.

00:10:14.009 --> 00:10:17.790
And the things that we're talking about today
in the United States are topical around the

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.240
entire country.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:25.110
Our focus today is -- and there's a lot of
discussion today about the idea of longevity

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:32.180
and living longer and trying to create products
that help people feel secure about their longevity

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.060
or their -- you know, like to your earlier
point, like, is it a bad thing that we're

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.750
actually going to live to 85 or beyond that?

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.700
I mean, this is the debate that we're having.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:43.819
And in the financial services market, of course,
we're trying to find solutions to help people

00:10:43.819 --> 00:10:45.560
feel good about those things.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:50.730
I think the debates that we see today with
respect to entitlement reform and all that

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:54.130
is a lot around people's insecurity around
what's going to happen in that future.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:58.649
And so it's incumbent upon our business market
to think through ways to help provide that

00:10:58.649 --> 00:11:05.350
type of security through programs that are
available from the early parts of an individual's

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:09.480
life, so that it's not something that they
start to think about when, arguably, we might

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:13.040
think it's too late to start thinking about
it or if there's not enough time.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:17.550
So we're challenged on a global basis, and
it's important to kind of take the lessons

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:21.660
that we learn in one market and transfer them
into the opportunity that it might create

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:22.660
in another market.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:28.470
So if you look at the European marketplace
and their philosophy around living and social

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:33.011
networks that provide for things that may
not necessarily, let's say, be available in

00:11:33.011 --> 00:11:36.410
the United States, it's important to try to
look at that and look for opportunities where

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:40.819
we can learn, but, more importantly, where
we can look for opportunities to enhance the

00:11:40.819 --> 00:11:48.060
life of our folks here in the United States
specifically, for my particular interest,

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:55.480
but more importantly, that we look for products
that can augment or forgo what may have happened

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.470
poorly in another marketplace.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.699
So we're spending a tremendous amount of time
trying to look for opportunities there.

00:12:00.699 --> 00:12:01.699
&gt;&gt;HODIN: So -- thank you.

00:12:01.699 --> 00:12:10.670
So speaking of Europe, as many of us will
know, 2012 -- Europe has announced that it

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:20.510
is the year of healthy and active aging, not
least, as Joe mentioned, because of this proportion

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:27.649
they now have to deal with of work/retirement,
if one looks at retirement in the traditional

00:12:27.649 --> 00:12:30.889
sense of over 60.

00:12:30.889 --> 00:12:33.389
I look around the room.

00:12:33.389 --> 00:12:39.620
Several of us came back from the first APEC
meeting in 2012, held in Moscow.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.070
It was cold.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:50.940
But APEC now has a strategy on active aging,
huge interest throughout particularly the

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:51.940
Asian region.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:59.060
We know that those dependency ratios in Taiwan,
China, Singapore, are going to be reaching

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:01.060
40 percent within the next couple decades.

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:09.560
It's not surprising, then, that the World
Health Organization has declared that 2012,

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:12.620
its World Health Day will be dedicated to
aging populations.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:20.680
So 2012 has sort of emerged as a year where
we look at this topic of aging populations,

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:27.889
and inside that, as we look here in the council
at what does it mean for America and our competitiveness

00:13:27.889 --> 00:13:36.139
and of foreign economic policy, I suppose
my question is, what is America's value proposition

00:13:36.139 --> 00:13:41.649
relative our trading partners, relative our
G-20 competitors?

00:13:41.649 --> 00:13:47.399
And how can we take some steps that could
model ourselves in a way that perhaps our

00:13:47.399 --> 00:13:51.749
European friends or friends in APEC and other
places will look to?

00:13:51.749 --> 00:13:53.829
Joe, if you could pick that one up.

00:13:53.829 --> 00:13:59.820
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: I'm going to give -- identify,
I think, two assets -- one tangible, one less

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:05.699
so, but I will argue that the less tangible
asset is America's greatest source of strategic

00:14:05.699 --> 00:14:07.209
advantage.

00:14:07.209 --> 00:14:13.540
We did a study with Gallup-Healthways Well-Being
Index to measure the well-being of adults

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:17.259
of any age, but we focused primarily on the
50-plus.

00:14:17.259 --> 00:14:21.829
And we looked at the well-being of older Germans,
the well-being of older folks in the U.K.

00:14:21.829 --> 00:14:24.149
and, of course, older Americans.

00:14:24.149 --> 00:14:29.339
And we found that the Germans were healthier
overall -- chronic disease and much more physically

00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:33.180
fit and walked and did all the things we should
do.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:37.370
The British had better access, in many cases,
to services, like National Health Service

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:38.990
and the like.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.790
And the Americans, you can tell by looking
at me, did not exercise as much.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.240
We thought that cheese steak was a food group.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.450
(Laughter.)

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:52.060
And on objective measures, we were not faring
as well in old age as our German and U.K.

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:53.060
counterparts.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.060
But guess what?

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:59.589
We blew the index off in terms of being in
a far higher state of well-being.

00:14:59.589 --> 00:15:04.839
That less tangible asset, that I would argue
is distinctly American -- some may say misguided,

00:15:04.839 --> 00:15:09.600
but I'll go with it -- is optimism; that the
older population in the United States had

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:15.490
a far greater sense of optimism that tomorrow
will be better than yesterday, where we will

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:17.100
find a way.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:21.709
So that optimism -- less tangible -- tied
to something I do think is more tangible,

00:15:21.709 --> 00:15:27.620
is that the U.S. has got a very strong base
of universities and industries developing

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:32.379
new technologies -- and technology without
service is just a commodity -- and services

00:15:32.379 --> 00:15:37.180
brought together to figure out how to live
longer better: how to stay in your home, how

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:41.439
to be able to remain in the car or the metro
system, how to stay in the workplace longer;

00:15:41.439 --> 00:15:46.820
to reinvent not just living longer, but how
to reinvent living entirely.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:51.089
So I'd say that the U.S. strategic advantage
is essentially at its core of its political

00:15:51.089 --> 00:15:55.110
culture: optimism, and that there's always
going to be a silver bullet to somehow make

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:56.110
it better.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:03.829
&gt;&gt;HODIN: So Kelly, as you look around the
world, working for a major global corporation,

00:16:03.829 --> 00:16:08.889
where are some of the countries that do have
a competitive advantage, or disadvantage,

00:16:08.889 --> 00:16:13.759
from the standpoint of aging populations,
and particularly since you're developing and

00:16:13.759 --> 00:16:16.839
designing financial instruments that are going
to be useful?

00:16:16.839 --> 00:16:20.380
You're looking at pension, you're looking
at retirement, you're looking at financial

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:21.380
security.

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:24.950
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: Well, a couple things.

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:32.129
If you think about just the United States
-- and I'll just use Europe as an example

00:16:32.129 --> 00:16:33.740
-- the European market is very DB (ph)-focused.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:39.259
They're very focused around the idea of the
social network.

00:16:39.259 --> 00:16:43.240
And while that still exists in the United
States, it's a very different culture, particularly

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.440
if you start looking at the younger ages.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.209
We're very defined-contribution oriented,
we're very self-reliant in many ways, and

00:16:50.209 --> 00:16:53.309
I think it's all a matter of how you look
at it.

00:16:53.309 --> 00:16:59.069
Right now we focus on that lack of -- it's
easy to get very mired in the idea that people

00:16:59.069 --> 00:17:06.660
don't have that same pension opportunity or
pension security blanket like the Europeans

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:08.480
look at, and look at that potentially as a
negative.

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:12.669
But I think particularly as you look at the
younger ages, that may ultimately end up being

00:17:12.669 --> 00:17:18.839
a significant advantage, in that we are culturally
already gearing ourselves toward this notion

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:22.799
of self-reliance and the need to save for
retirement.

00:17:22.799 --> 00:17:27.220
In fact, Jan and I -- Janet Servonce (ph)
from AEGON -- and I were talking a little

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:28.220
bit.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.659
We're conducting a study right now between
the nuances and differences in attitudes between

00:17:31.659 --> 00:17:35.950
the U.S. and the EU with respect to retirement.

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:40.260
And one of the things that is -- the questions
that we want to understand is how people feel

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:43.300
about the ability to stay in their homes.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:49.810
And the folks that were participating in the
EU side of the survey were sort of completely

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:55.240
baffled by the idea of having to potentially
lose their home.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:18:00.770
That just doesn't even occur in their lexicon
of concerns.

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:08.380
And their questions were more centered around
the loss of happiness or the loss of self-worth

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:09.380
and what not.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:10.890
It's a completely different paradigm.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:15.480
So the degree that -- I suppose it's a matter
of how you're looking at the glass, but the

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:20.659
idea that we're very self-reliant and that
we have this culture of self-reliance in the

00:18:20.659 --> 00:18:24.649
U.S., I think, while we look at the shortcomings,
that we're not there yet, we have a lot of

00:18:24.649 --> 00:18:29.500
work to do to get people to be financially
secure; the notion that the average man is

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:33.309
actually thinking about that, I think, is
a substantial competitive advantage.

00:18:33.309 --> 00:18:37.480
And I think I'd like to add to your comments
the fact that U.S., particularly the entrepreneurship

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:42.660
of the U.S., cannot be underestimated as it
relates to trying to solve this problem, to

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:45.590
bring us to some innovative conclusions.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:50.059
And, you know, working with government to
try to create that environment, I think will

00:18:50.059 --> 00:18:51.640
help us tremendously in the long run.

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:52.640
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: Yeah.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:56.060
Especially if you look at some of the Kauffman
Foundation data, it suggests that the next-generation

00:18:56.060 --> 00:19:01.159
entrepreneur is not someone who's 20-something
from MIT or Stanford and Silicon Valley but

00:19:01.159 --> 00:19:05.090
rather is more likely to be someone 45 and
older, and more likely to be a woman.

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:08.529
So as I look in the mirror every day and aging
becomes a greater reality --

00:19:08.529 --> 00:19:09.529
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: You could be talking about me.

00:19:09.529 --> 00:19:10.529
I love that!

00:19:10.529 --> 00:19:11.529
(Laughs.)

00:19:11.529 --> 00:19:12.529
MR.

00:19:12.529 --> 00:19:13.529
: -- I need that entrepreneur.

00:19:13.529 --> 00:19:21.610
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Well, as we get very close to opening
up to the full discussion, let's -- our proposition

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:29.570
here is really, in a way, very simple, but
many will look at it as ranging from naive

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:32.919
to idealistic to unrealizable.

00:19:32.919 --> 00:19:42.090
And it is simply that in our 21st century,
we've now, over the last century, added roughly

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:44.140
three decades to life.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:49.620
That's certainly true in America, and it's
true largely around the G-20.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:56.929
So if we're going to live into our 90s, the
idea of being -- having a work-retirement

00:19:56.929 --> 00:20:04.730
structure and public policies aligned to that
-- that was built, by the way, off of the

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:11.580
Bismarck model in the 19th century, and operating
through the 20th century -- how in the world

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:15.030
can that be applicable to the 21st?

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:22.049
Yet, whether we're talking about Wisconsin,
the budget that was just put out yesterday

00:20:22.049 --> 00:20:31.960
-- I guess this direction -- or Greece, politics
certainly seems to get in the way.

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:32.960
How do we manage this?

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:44.970
How do we find our way toward a future where
the aging population begins to be understood

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:50.460
as engaging in economic activity, part of
a growth driver?

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:58.011
You know, that's a -- such a huge sociological
and cultural shift that it's almost how do

00:20:58.011 --> 00:21:00.370
you get your mind around that?

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:02.230
What is the realism for that?

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:03.760
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: How do we get it done?

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:04.760
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Yes.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:07.799
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: At least it's an easy question,
right?

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:11.120
I think -- and this is a conversation that
Mike and I have had offline as well, but I

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:19.090
think we now really need to have a phased
redefinition of our social contract with -- as

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:21.880
individuals, with governments, individuals
with each other and the like.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:27.720
We cannot use yesterday's policies when life
expectancy was only 45 to 50 to redefine our

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:28.720
future 50 years.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:35.270
So that means yes, working longer is a new
reality, not just to hedge financial problems,

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:38.250
but frankly, working longer is healthier for
you as well.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:41.890
But before we just say that's all about the
individual, institutions need to change.

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:46.309
The workplace needs to be redesigned physically
as well as perhaps ideologically at how we

00:21:46.309 --> 00:21:49.850
look at older workers, how we look at education.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:53.929
We need to rethink in terms of what does old
age mean in terms of what your contribution

00:21:53.929 --> 00:21:54.929
is going to be.

00:21:54.929 --> 00:21:57.029
Is it to families alone?

00:21:57.029 --> 00:21:58.029
Is it to yourself?

00:21:58.029 --> 00:22:00.020
It is to those around you?

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:04.230
So I think that the real change has got to
be about how to look at that next 30 years

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:09.149
not just as a personal dividend of living
longer, but a dividend that society needs

00:22:09.149 --> 00:22:13.270
to also equally benefit from, since it also
invested in it.

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:14.270
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Great.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:15.270
Kelly?

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:18.420
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: I would add that -- I completely
agree, and I would really add to that that

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:25.761
we have to rethink about how we educate people
in this respect, because I can tell you, 20-some

00:22:25.761 --> 00:22:31.510
years I've been working in the financial services
market and thinking about education and walking

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:34.890
into a workplace and talking about retirement.

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:39.740
For many, it's the first time they've ever
had a conversation about saving in their entire

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:40.740
life.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:43.789
And that's not just the youngest of employees;
that's a lot of the employees.

00:22:43.789 --> 00:22:50.029
And so for our fundamental understanding of
how we live to change, how we educate people

00:22:50.029 --> 00:22:52.020
about this is going to have to change.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:56.220
And whose responsibility that is to educate
these people has to change.

00:22:56.220 --> 00:23:02.669
And so to the degree that this can be begun
at a much younger age in a different -- in

00:23:02.669 --> 00:23:06.740
a way that is not meant to be something that
sits on its own -- it should be integrated

00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:10.299
into all of the things that we do -- then
you'll find -- I think that we'll see the

00:23:10.299 --> 00:23:16.549
workplace change more so than it -- I just
don't think we can do it with the same sort

00:23:16.549 --> 00:23:20.929
of understanding that we have today about
the importance of living longer and how to

00:23:20.929 --> 00:23:23.140
sustain yourself through that.

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:26.090
It really has to have a big educational component
to it.

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:29.429
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: The near normal, I think, has
-- the new normal has to be reinforced, if

00:23:29.429 --> 00:23:30.460
you will, by peer pressure.

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:34.269
You know, it worked when we were teenagers;
it'll work through midlife and older life.

00:23:34.269 --> 00:23:36.289
What is it that we expect from each other?

00:23:36.289 --> 00:23:42.110
And before, we used to expect retirement -- 55,
60, 62 years old was the normal.

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:43.889
That's not going to be sustainable.

00:23:43.889 --> 00:23:46.690
But how do we reinforce it amongst each other?

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:47.960
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Thank you.

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:53.880
So hopefully, we've at least set a little
bit of a context, perhaps gotten the juices

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:55.779
flowing a little bit intellectually.

00:23:55.779 --> 00:23:59.039
We'd now like to open it up.

00:23:59.039 --> 00:24:02.110
Just raise your hand -- yes -- and we'll call
on you.

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:04.559
There will be a microphone.

00:24:04.559 --> 00:24:09.860
You can either direct your questions or just
throw it out there.

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:13.750
And if you want to make a comment, that's
fine too, but do limit it so others have a

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:14.750
chance.

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:15.750
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:16.750
I'm Steven Koltai.

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:19.340
I run the Global Entrepreneurship Program
at the State Department.

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:28.190
I have a question about the size of the contingent
liability for pensions and Social Security

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:34.390
payments and how that compares between the
United States, Europe and Japan.

00:24:34.390 --> 00:24:41.320
And what I'm especially interested in is not
just the public sector size -- so in other

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:48.000
words, not just the Social Security piece
-- but also the private sector pension liability.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:52.100
And from an economic competitiveness standpoint,
I'm curious how that compares between the

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:53.100
U.S., Europe and Japan.

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:56.090
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: You mean -- I'm sorry, I just want
to make sure I understand the question.

00:24:56.090 --> 00:24:57.929
You want to know how big it is --

00:24:57.929 --> 00:24:58.929
QUESTIONER: Yes.

00:24:58.929 --> 00:24:59.929
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: -- compared to other countries?

00:24:59.929 --> 00:25:00.929
QUESTIONER: Yes.

00:25:00.929 --> 00:25:03.220
In other words, I want to know whether we
are advantaged or disadvantaged in the U.S.

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:07.330
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: I don't know if I have the exact
statistic -- and I apologize for that; I can

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:11.240
certainly get that for you -- of what the
actual total market is.

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:15.950
But I want to say it's, in aggregate, if you
look at defined contribution and defined benefit,

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:21.110
somewhere upwards around the $4 trillion -- and
I don't know if I can give you an accurate

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:27.019
comparison to another country, but I think
what's -- to the topic, I think what's most

00:25:27.019 --> 00:25:33.460
important is the ability for people to rely
on that compared to other countries.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:38.240
And maybe I'm not getting at the heart of
what you're interested in, but from my perspective,

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:45.710
our -- in the United States, I think the typical
-- outside of your home, the typical total

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:49.899
savings on average is around $72,000 right
now.

00:25:49.899 --> 00:25:54.799
And if you look at that as a means to measure
whether you're financially secure or not,

00:25:54.799 --> 00:25:57.960
I think it doesn't take someone on a panel
to tell you that that's probably not close

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.529
enough to what you are going to probably need.

00:26:00.529 --> 00:26:04.880
So to that end, compared to some other countries,
we may be behind.

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:13.419
But I think in large part, there is a tremendous
amount of support toward the -- of course,

00:26:13.419 --> 00:26:18.909
the increasing of that pool of dollars, but
doing it in such a way where the individual

00:26:18.909 --> 00:26:24.120
is really driving that component, which -- and
depends on how you look at it, whether that's

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.950
an advantage or a disadvantage.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:26.950
I don't know.

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:27.950
Joe, you want to add something to that?

00:26:27.950 --> 00:26:30.149
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: Yeah, I look at those numbers
in terms of per capita -- you know, how many

00:26:30.149 --> 00:26:34.929
legs to their Social Security stool that they
have.

00:26:34.929 --> 00:26:39.510
There may be many more supports, for instance,
in a -- in a social welfare state.

00:26:39.510 --> 00:26:44.590
But now we're starting to find that both the
trust of the viability of that -- those supports

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:45.590
may be in question.

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:47.020
So I'd lean on that.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:52.480
&gt;&gt;HODIN: And the only thing I would add to
that is when we look -- when we draw our lines

00:26:52.480 --> 00:27:00.549
out the next two and three decades, across
the G-20 countries, and you start getting

00:27:00.549 --> 00:27:06.670
upwards of 40 percent dependency ratios in
some countries, the numbers become unsustainable.

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:12.799
I'm also very worried -- there are some very
fine experts in a number of these topics among

00:27:12.799 --> 00:27:18.779
all of you out there, so if anyone has a particular
data point or fact that you want to throw

00:27:18.779 --> 00:27:21.220
out, please raise your hand on that one.

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:22.789
We're not the experts.

00:27:22.789 --> 00:27:23.789
Danley (ph).

00:27:23.789 --> 00:27:27.289
QUESTIONER: Donmer Haskul (ph), the Social
Security Administration.

00:27:27.289 --> 00:27:34.160
When we look at the comparative weight or
the burden of the aging in the future, some

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:37.470
countries come out very well, and quite unexpectedly:
Sweden, Italy, Poland.

00:27:37.470 --> 00:27:43.260
And these are countries which have tied the
retirement age to life expectancy.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:44.950
It's in the law.

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:49.279
And that makes a tremendous difference when
you're projecting those numbers out 20, 30

00:27:49.279 --> 00:27:50.279
years.

00:27:50.279 --> 00:27:55.059
Now, the question is going to be a political
question: Will they let the law work and raise

00:27:55.059 --> 00:27:58.500
the retirement age to 70, 72, 73?

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:04.320
The Swedish prime minister just said 75, and
it caused a press uproar.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:05.320
(Laughter.)

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:11.399
So you know, it may be in the law, but will
the political process let it run out?

00:28:11.399 --> 00:28:15.620
But it is one way to tackle the burden.

00:28:15.620 --> 00:28:20.399
&gt;&gt;HODIN: And notwithstanding the firebombs
in the streets of Paris that we saw when President

00:28:20.399 --> 00:28:27.570
Sarkozy made his announcements about a year
and a half ago with respect to the age, he

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:31.350
did make progress on tying it to how long
we live.

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:34.770
Again, whether that will be implemented or
not is another story.

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:37.950
There were -- yes, in the back.

00:28:37.950 --> 00:28:41.009
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: Perhaps we need to tie it to the
-- most people, when you ask them what old

00:28:41.009 --> 00:28:43.460
age is, it's always 15 to 20 years older than
they are.

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:45.710
That's what we should tie retirement to.

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:46.710
(Laughter.)

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:50.790
QUESTIONER: I'm Welbry Lyman (ph) from Ways
and Means.

00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:57.480
You talked a bit about the potential, the
consumption potential of an aging American

00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:07.020
population, and also mentioned just briefly
the potential for productive -- you know,

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:08.799
extended productive capacity.

00:29:08.799 --> 00:29:11.150
Can you describe a bit how that works?

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:18.870
What are the types of jobs that we can be
globally competitive in as an aging population?

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:23.840
And does this prioritize services over manufacturing,
for example?

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:32.789
And if so, what does that do to the sort of
generational mix of jobs?

00:29:32.789 --> 00:29:37.080
Do we keep -- does everyone in this audience
basically hold onto their jobs for an extra

00:29:37.080 --> 00:29:42.460
10 years, and then the folks underneath have
a new ceiling that they can't reach?

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:44.409
Do we need to do something for them?

00:29:44.409 --> 00:29:47.110
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: Like most things from House and
Means (sic), that's got about five or six

00:29:47.110 --> 00:29:48.870
intertwined questions in there.

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:49.870
(Laughter.)

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:50.870
That -- a great question.

00:29:50.870 --> 00:29:55.889
First off, I think it plays to America's strategic
advantage even today, which is we don't manufacture

00:29:55.889 --> 00:30:00.250
everything; we tend to manage the more high-quality,
high-craft jobs.

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:01.250
And guess what?

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:07.240
Whether you're talking about BMW or the high-end
product manufacturing of, you know, IT devices

00:30:07.240 --> 00:30:14.179
and the like, it tends to be the 45- to 50-plus-year-old
worker who either knows exactly what that

00:30:14.179 --> 00:30:19.010
craft is -- so exactly how to, say, get a
dashboard into a very expensive vehicle -- or

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:21.610
how a system should be manufactured in IT.

00:30:21.610 --> 00:30:26.790
So, yeah, there may be a bias in services,
but not necessarily.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:31.899
The 50-plus, whether we like to admit or not
when we are under 50, know what organizations

00:30:31.899 --> 00:30:32.970
or companies know.

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:36.860
So there's going to be a bias to high-quality/high-value
product.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:39.870
With respect to the folks who actually work
for a living -- that is, those who use their

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:44.929
bodies and carry bricks and the like -- we
are seeing great advances, particularly in

00:30:44.929 --> 00:30:49.940
Europe, around cobotics and robotics being
able to enable people to stay in the workplace

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:50.940
longer.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:56.450
And lastly -- and I'll yield to Kelly -- is
the notion that there's this low -- long line

00:30:56.450 --> 00:31:00.740
of young people banging on the door for the
job, waiting for you to get of your way -- get

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:06.350
out of the way, is true in some areas, but
in most areas, particularly in high tech,

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:07.669
it is not the case.

00:31:07.669 --> 00:31:12.470
We have a tremendous shortage of people in
government service, engineering, air traffic

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:13.470
controls.

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:16.190
If anyone flew here, you should know that
the average air traffic controller is closer

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:18.450
to 60 than they are to 40.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:20.330
And we don't have anyone taking those jobs.

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:23.549
So we really need to keep folks as long as
possible.

00:31:23.549 --> 00:31:27.470
And I guess -- and the last point is that
hopefully none of us will stay in the same

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:29.250
job for 30 or 40 years.

00:31:29.250 --> 00:31:33.379
Hopefully we will reinvent institutions and
our own expectations to have three or four

00:31:33.379 --> 00:31:37.279
different careers in a 60- to 70-year lifespan
of work.

00:31:37.279 --> 00:31:41.610
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: I think the average life insurance
salesman is also in the same situation as

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:42.610
your --

00:31:42.610 --> 00:31:43.610
MR.

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:44.610
: Yeah.

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:45.620
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: -- as your -- as your high-tech
person.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:51.549
But I would add to the comments that you just
made -- were that the -- whether you stay

00:31:51.549 --> 00:31:56.240
in -- whether you're in a job that can stay
-- that you can sustain for an extra 10 years

00:31:56.240 --> 00:32:00.179
or an extra 15 years has a lot to do with
the institution or the industry that you're

00:32:00.179 --> 00:32:05.440
in and their value that they place on the
intellectual capital that you have in that

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:06.440
job.

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:10.639
So I think to your point, if you're a bricklayer,
there's a point where your physical stamina

00:32:10.639 --> 00:32:12.980
can't keep up with that job at an older age.

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:17.370
But to the degree that you're in a professional
service then -- and that your employer wants

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:21.029
to retain that intellectual capital, it's
going to be very important to know whether

00:32:21.029 --> 00:32:24.230
or not you're going to have to find a new
career or not.

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:27.860
Moreover, I think it's important to see that
those companies or those industries that do

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:32.481
value that intellectual capital keep -- that
-- again, getting back to, like, the need

00:32:32.481 --> 00:32:38.620
for the educational reinvestment in our people
-- that industry, you would hope, is going

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:44.360
to continue to invest in their -- in their
employees' educational ability to continue

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:45.970
to be competitive.

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:56.169
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Yes, I would just highlight the point
that there aren't that many -- unfortunately

00:32:56.169 --> 00:33:04.090
there aren't that many places that we can
turn to right now to give great best practices.

00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:12.149
But the few that exist, there are at least
as many that are in the -- what we might consider

00:33:12.149 --> 00:33:15.049
blue-collar versus white-collar arena.

00:33:15.049 --> 00:33:18.029
And you mentioned BMW factory workers.

00:33:18.029 --> 00:33:22.000
Harley-Davidson is another -- some of you
may have seen some pieces on -- which are

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:25.799
doing very interesting and innovative work.

00:33:25.799 --> 00:33:31.680
And when you combine that, as Joe mentioned,
with the technology component, we -- we're

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:35.640
going to have to break down all kinds of assumptions
over the next couple decades.

00:33:35.640 --> 00:33:42.149
So, you know, this is as much about a culture
and sociological shift as anything else.

00:33:42.149 --> 00:33:44.020
Tom, you had a question.

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:46.030
We'll try to get to everyone.

00:33:46.030 --> 00:33:47.309
We'll move it around quickly.

00:33:47.309 --> 00:33:51.900
QUESTIONER: Tom Miller, I work -- I head an
-- oh, excuse me -- Tom Miller, I head an

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:56.120
organization called International Executive
Service Corps, which is a nonprofit, and we

00:33:56.120 --> 00:34:01.100
have about 11,000 volunteers, most of them
retired.

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:04.990
And my question kind of follows up on what
you were just talking about.

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:06.800
It's a specific question for you, Kelly.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:13.570
What is -- what is AEGON doing specifically
for older people in terms of integrating or

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:16.060
keeping them in the workforce?

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:23.870
And Joe, it's more -- you know, you talk about
changing the culture, and I'd like to get

00:34:23.870 --> 00:34:30.260
-- you know, we've talked to a lot of companies,
and it's hard to get them to think that people

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:34.160
over 65 can still contribute to the workforce.

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:42.330
And once you get to that, they say, well,
then we keep people younger from advancing.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:44.650
And so, Mike, I'd like you to maybe deal with
that.

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:48.440
And our colleague from the State Department
-- I was at the State Department for 30 years,

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:51.190
and we used to have these questions there.

00:34:51.190 --> 00:34:59.270
And you know, you always got hung up with,
well, you know, you would really destroy morale

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:04.700
and a lot (other ?) things if you kind of
kept people over a certain age.

00:35:04.700 --> 00:35:09.391
&gt;&gt;HODIN (?): Well, just on the last point,
to jump in, I mean, the example that I always

00:35:09.391 --> 00:35:12.080
like to use is the women's movement.

00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:16.470
I mean, there was a time -- some of us can
remember -- at the beginning of the women's

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:20.150
movement when it was assumed that that was
awful because it would take -- you know, in

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:24.050
those days, I was younger, but -- my job.

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:25.050
Wasn't true.

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:30.320
If -- you know, it's basically the point if
you have a growing economy, if you can look

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:34.650
at growth and opportunity, then you've got
an expanding pie.

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:40.210
If you do have a pie that you're, you know,
cutting up among -- and it's limited, then

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:43.000
you're going to have beggar thy neighbor.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:46.450
But that's not the place that America has
been, and that's not the place that America

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:47.450
has to be.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:51.450
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN (?): (I think ?) also there's bureaucratic
realities.

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:55.620
Corporations are -- have split personalities,
much like government agencies do.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:36:00.280
So while you're asking for early outs for
your 55-plus or even 60-year-olds that go

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:05.050
out the door, the head of human resources
and the chief financial officer are all throwing

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:06.050
a party.

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:07.050
They've checked that box.

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:11.050
They've costed -- they've downsized, and they've
saved money.

00:36:11.050 --> 00:36:17.540
The technology officer or the chief of product
development and the like is going, if I only

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:19.080
knew what we know.

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:23.880
And by the way, the person who just walked
out took with her the relationship with our

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:29.670
client or took with him how we actually deliver
a given set of services.

00:36:29.670 --> 00:36:33.550
And so there's a split personality in there
in terms of how organizations want some people

00:36:33.550 --> 00:36:35.970
to get out quickly, so we can bring in new
talent.

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:40.570
On the other hand, we also know that the folks
that sit, you know -- in their heads and in

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:43.810
their drawers are the things that power and
knowledge is made of.

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:47.250
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: I agree with that, and I'll just
make one final comment.

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:48.250
You had asked about AEGON.

00:36:48.250 --> 00:36:54.330
AEGON is a -- has a tremendous program to
keep and reinvest in their own staff.

00:36:54.330 --> 00:36:58.800
But I think more importantly the comments
that I would make about this topic are relative

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:02.430
to the clients and the organizations that
we serve -- are -- which are everything from

00:37:02.430 --> 00:37:06.430
very small, micro-sized employers to very
large employers.

00:37:06.430 --> 00:37:11.080
And by and large, these are -- of course,
to your point of sort of organizational sort

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:18.780
of reality that employees sort of come and
go, and it may be lots of reasons why they

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:24.550
may do that -- but most companies don't make
decisions about to keep somebody or to let

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:26.460
someone go based strictly on age.

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.520
It could -- there's all sorts of factors around
that.

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:33.030
And I think, again, going back to reinventing
or reinvesting in those people as products

00:37:33.030 --> 00:37:38.120
and innovation changes, that comes oftentimes
not just by young people, it comes with a

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:41.780
lot of experience and thoughtfulness around
how the marketplace actually works.

00:37:41.780 --> 00:37:47.900
So the loss of intellectual capital at -- for
our older employees is -- it will be just

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:49.270
that, in fact, a really huge loss.

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:52.390
&gt;&gt;HODIN: And one of the components that we
haven't talked about that -- just so -- to

00:37:52.390 --> 00:38:02.060
throw out -- the -- being realistic, there
is -- there are health factors.

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:09.010
You know, the kind of numbers that we're looking
at -- with Alzheimer's, diabetes, cardiovascular

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:15.070
and cancer -- will be our nightmare of this
century.

00:38:15.070 --> 00:38:21.910
Alzheimer's alone, many of you know, is 604
billion, 1 percent of global GDP, and we're

00:38:21.910 --> 00:38:25.270
confident that that number is understated
and that's today.

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:33.390
With risks of getting it 1-in-8 when you're
over 65 and 1-in-2 1/2 when you're over 85,

00:38:33.390 --> 00:38:35.620
this is a 21st century nightmare, as I said.

00:38:35.620 --> 00:38:38.520
And you look at diabetes and cancer, it's
the same thing.

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:43.680
So, you know, we can have all the great intentions,
we can have the right kind of programs, we

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:50.150
can even begin to count an aging workforce
as part of diversity, but if we don't address

00:38:50.150 --> 00:38:53.590
the health problems, that's going to be an
issue as well.

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:59.790
And that's something America is particularly
well-suited to, I think, drive on the innovation

00:38:59.790 --> 00:39:01.730
side.

00:39:01.730 --> 00:39:06.590
I think it's probably -- rather than trying
to answer every question -- and all of three

00:39:06.590 --> 00:39:11.461
of us, why don't we take four or five together
and then we can try to have -- so Mitzi (sp)

00:39:11.461 --> 00:39:17.190
and then Chris (sp) and you were -- let's
say you go next, and then right there.

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:18.190
And then --

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:21.660
QUESTIONER: I actually have two questions.

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:27.820
I guess one is that we have a corporate desire
for efficiency, which always means getting

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:28.820
rid of people.

00:39:28.820 --> 00:39:35.180
I mean, it -- I spent 13 years at IBM; I mean,
I just watched all this happen there.

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:38.100
The other is the opposite end of the population.

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:45.320
I don't know; are we talking about 20 percent,
30 percent, 40 percent of the working population

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:48.160
that aren't a part of these institutions where
they can go on?

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:52.740
I mean, it's -- some of them are the working
poor, and some of them are just the poor.

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:54.030
And how do we deal with them?

00:39:54.030 --> 00:40:03.030
And I guess the last question is, I don't
want to have a lingering death, but it's illegal

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:07.980
for my daughter to get me pills so I could
basically bring my life to an end.

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:09.610
How do we deal with something like that?

00:40:09.610 --> 00:40:13.870
Those of us that would -- I mean, I just don't
want a lingering death.

00:40:13.870 --> 00:40:14.870
(Chuckles.)

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:16.500
&gt;&gt;HODIN (?): A good question.

00:40:16.500 --> 00:40:17.500
Let's -- Chris, did you --

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:23.590
QUESTIONER: On the heels of that question,
everything seems trivial, I'm afraid.

00:40:23.590 --> 00:40:24.590
(Laughter.)

00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:26.680
My name is Chris Graves with Ogilvy.

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:32.600
And linking on from IBM, IBM learned that
while their -- surprisingly, their mainframes

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:36.420
did not die as predicted, the people who know
how to maintain them did.

00:40:36.420 --> 00:40:37.420
(Laughter.)

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:41.930
So nobody young and cool wants to work on
them, which brings me to my question for you.

00:40:41.930 --> 00:40:46.520
Are we so obsessed with youth culture that
there's an enormous gap in consumer products

00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:48.060
addressing the aging?

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:53.450
I noticed your very cool watch -- (laughter)
-- and I wondered if that MIT AgeLab, whether

00:40:53.450 --> 00:40:57.810
there -- you see any hope there of filling
that gap.

00:40:57.810 --> 00:41:03.030
And is the obstacle what you alluded to earlier,
that none of us wants to admit that we reach

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:06.670
for the reading glasses and the ibuprofen
every day -- (laughter) -- and so we always

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:10.960
consider ourselves 30 years younger than we
really are, thus marketing aging products

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:11.960
to us is very difficult?

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:13.300
&gt;&gt;HODIN: You have to show them your glasses
-- (inaudible) --

00:41:13.300 --> 00:41:14.614
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: I was just noticing that -- Mitzi
(sp) -- I just want to say, before you answer

00:41:14.614 --> 00:41:16.580
the question, Mitzi (sp), I was just noticing
your nametag.

00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:19.741
It's very large, "Mitzi," so you're the only
person I can name, actually -- (chuckles)

00:41:19.741 --> 00:41:20.780
-- in the entire crowd.

00:41:20.780 --> 00:41:22.000
And I appreciate that.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.170
QUESTIONER: (Off mic.)

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:24.730
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Thank you, Chris.

00:41:24.730 --> 00:41:30.950
I think -- sir, yes?

00:41:30.950 --> 00:41:35.240
This also gives us a chance to actually pick
certain questions to answer and avoid others,

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:38.210
like whether you should die when you're 95
or not.

00:41:38.210 --> 00:41:39.210
(Laughter.)

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:45.280
QUESTIONER: Could you please comment on the
financial support required to sustain the

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:51.140
two principal vehicles for supporting the
aging population in the U.S., namely Medicare

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:52.670
and Social Security?

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:58.580
Both seem to be inadequately financed at the
moment.

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:00.940
And this problem looks as though it will get
worse.

00:42:00.940 --> 00:42:08.700
Do you believe that means testing is the way
to go about sustaining these support services?

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:10.150
Should it come out of general revenue?

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:13.960
Should we increase the contributions from
the working population?

00:42:13.960 --> 00:42:17.020
How do we make them financially viable?

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:21.540
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Great question and very relevant.

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:23.130
Let's keep them coming.

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:25.020
QUESTIONER: Hi, Rob Cortel (sp) with Initelics
(sp).

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:28.770
I actually have a foreign policy question.

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:35.030
Attitudes about public policy are often experiential,
not logical.

00:42:35.030 --> 00:42:38.120
So we have a generation that's disappearing
from World War II.

00:42:38.120 --> 00:42:42.200
I think the last World War I vet just died
a couple days ago.

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:47.490
They came out of the war with a certain set
of experiences, the Marshall fund and things

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:51.980
like that, which led to a more internationalist
point of view, and then we had the Soviets

00:42:51.980 --> 00:42:52.980
and all that.

00:42:52.980 --> 00:42:57.810
As this population, the American population,
ages, it'd be interesting to -- I'd like to

00:42:57.810 --> 00:43:02.350
understand what you are seeing in attitudes
about foreign policy and the U.S. engagement

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:06.490
in that, if we are in fact still the dominant
power in foreign policy.

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:10.920
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Good question.

00:43:10.920 --> 00:43:15.360
Yes, Katherine (sp).

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:17.050
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:22.570
I'm interested in the potential of older adults
to solve social problems.

00:43:22.570 --> 00:43:28.500
Often we think of late life as a roleless
phase of life where when you go to a party,

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:31.850
you have no answer to the question "what do
you do."

00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:37.210
So how can we reposition aging as a real time
of contribution, where older adults are looked

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:42.540
at as real contributors to solving social
problems and there's an infrastructure in

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:45.840
place to make that easy for them to do that?

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:50.300
So many volunteer opportunities seem piddly,
you know, where you're licking envelopes.

00:43:50.300 --> 00:43:54.800
They're not really tapping into your worth
as a -- and experience as a person.

00:43:54.800 --> 00:43:57.400
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Zavin (ph).

00:43:57.400 --> 00:44:00.160
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: Are you memorizing all these?

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:02.020
Because my brain can't hold them -- I'm writing
them down.

00:44:02.020 --> 00:44:03.020
(Laughter.)

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:04.740
We're going to start answering them.

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:10.920
QUESTIONER: One of the issues, I think -- I
spent most of my career at the National Institute

00:44:10.920 --> 00:44:17.970
on Aging, where we struggled with one of the
key issues, that is, the public perception

00:44:17.970 --> 00:44:19.540
of what aging is about.

00:44:19.540 --> 00:44:27.640
I think there needs to be a distinction between
chronological age and functional age.

00:44:27.640 --> 00:44:34.940
The Bismarck model was based on chronological
age and didn't take into account the functional

00:44:34.940 --> 00:44:36.070
aging.

00:44:36.070 --> 00:44:42.350
As an example, in the late '70s the National
Institute on Aging worked with the FAA trying

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:49.410
to change the retirement age for pilots at
age 60, where it was automatic, without taking

00:44:49.410 --> 00:44:53.570
into account whether a pilot could continue
functioning.

00:44:53.570 --> 00:44:59.890
So I think that should be the -- perhaps one
of the challenges is how do we change public

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:06.790
policy where it's more based on a person's
ability to continue functioning well.

00:45:06.790 --> 00:45:14.500
As longevity revolution ticks on, we're going
to see tremendous heterogeneity in the ability

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:16.170
of people to function.

00:45:16.170 --> 00:45:19.510
There are many 80-year-olds that can function
quite well.

00:45:19.510 --> 00:45:23.850
They function as well as some 40-year-olds
or 50-year-olds.

00:45:23.850 --> 00:45:30.870
So I think that's probably a critical issue
in how we design public policies to become

00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:38.380
more based on functional ability of a person
to continue working or to continue functioning,

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:39.380
whether it be with --

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:40.400
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: That's where technology plays
a role ---

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:41.900
QUESTIONER: Technology plays a role.

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:42.900
MR.

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:43.900
: -- extending the functionality.

00:45:43.900 --> 00:45:44.900
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Thank you, Zavin (ph).

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:46.440
So why don't we start answering some of these?

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:50.140
Joe -- (chuckles) -- perhaps start with Zavin's
(ph) point, which I think is -- captures --

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:54.480
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: Yeah, I think I was going to say,
you recall a number of years ago, did a show,

00:45:54.480 --> 00:45:57.900
old memory of baby boomers and defense policy
gone past.

00:45:57.900 --> 00:46:02.020
The notion of technology was always considered
a force multiplier in military policy, you

00:46:02.020 --> 00:46:05.850
know, one soldier -- good technology is five
soldiers on the field.

00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:10.530
We're now looking at technology as fundamentally
a force multiplier for either providing care

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:15.340
or for providing improved performance behind
the wheel of a car, in the workplace or being

00:46:15.340 --> 00:46:17.340
able to stay at home.

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:20.480
So I think functional age is incredibly important.

00:46:20.480 --> 00:46:22.200
We will be all chronically ill.

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:26.200
Probably most us in this room have at least
one chronic disease, or you've been ignoring

00:46:26.200 --> 00:46:29.140
your doctor, and you don't know that you probably
have two.

00:46:29.140 --> 00:46:32.440
So it's not a matter of how sick you're going
to be; the question is whether you'll be ill,

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:35.090
but still be in the game.

00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:39.520
To take the question about ageism, if you
will, and product development, I've got good

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:42.440
news and bad news in that department, the
cool watch aside.

00:46:42.440 --> 00:46:45.410
And I couldn't find my glasses, ironically.

00:46:45.410 --> 00:46:46.500
(Laughter.)

00:46:46.500 --> 00:46:51.231
I had my glass case -- a number of my students
are tired of me losing my glasses; they've

00:46:51.231 --> 00:46:56.890
put a radio frequency identification tag on
my case so that they can go on the computer

00:46:56.890 --> 00:46:59.830
and say, Joe, your glasses are wherever I've
left them.

00:46:59.830 --> 00:47:00.830
(Laughter.)

00:47:00.830 --> 00:47:03.480
The good news and bad news -- and this -- I
have noticed this even in Europe, where there

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:08.300
had tended to be a celebration of how bald
I can be in terms of aging.

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:12.940
And around the world, we're starting to find
that youth culture is perhaps one of America's

00:47:12.940 --> 00:47:14.540
greatest exports.

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:21.910
That said, no one wants to make or build an
old man's service or product.

00:47:21.910 --> 00:47:26.290
And the reason for that is the following:
because a young man or a young woman will

00:47:26.290 --> 00:47:28.600
not buy it or touch it or look at it.

00:47:28.600 --> 00:47:30.360
But guess what?

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:32.240
Neither will an old man or an older woman.

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:37.010
And so one of the contributions of an aging
society that has got an attitude that it's

00:47:37.010 --> 00:47:42.910
all about me and has more discretionary income
even after the downturn than any other cohort

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:47.680
is you are going to have a cool watch that
I'm wearing because, frankly, I can see it

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:50.410
without my glasses, and I can personalize
it.

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:54.440
I'm going to have something that's safer because
I'm frailer, but 20-year-olds don't complain

00:47:54.440 --> 00:47:55.770
about safety.

00:47:55.770 --> 00:48:00.470
And because I have a youthful mindset, I want
something that I can personalize, that's safe

00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:04.670
and easy to use that all of a sudden becomes
ageless.

00:48:04.670 --> 00:48:09.230
The notion of creating an old man's world
is neither competitive nor attractive.

00:48:09.230 --> 00:48:13.400
And so I think one of the great contributions
will be that the next generation of old will

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:18.530
make a generation of better, easier-used,
more fun living experience in the future.

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:21.110
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: And I think that goes to your question,
too, Katherine (sp).

00:48:21.110 --> 00:48:22.790
And I don't know if that's what you were asking.

00:48:22.790 --> 00:48:25.950
But really, the -- it's already happening,
I think, to some degree.

00:48:25.950 --> 00:48:31.900
And again, this is more taking a very positive
notation over this discussion, right?

00:48:31.900 --> 00:48:37.680
I think that we're maybe a lot -- very focused
on the negative aspects of having this aging

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:42.300
population, but the fact is is that that older
American or older worker that really wants

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:47.140
to participate is going to be amongst their
own cohort deciding how to live out their

00:48:47.140 --> 00:48:48.140
life.

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:54.620
And they're not going to be the -- I think
the notion of what an aged population means

00:48:54.620 --> 00:48:58.870
is changing as we talk today and that there
will be other opportunities, and it's really

00:48:58.870 --> 00:49:01.560
to the degree that they wish to continue to
be engaged.

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:04.270
And they won't have to just be, hopefully,
licking envelopes.

00:49:04.270 --> 00:49:07.560
And if that's -- if that is what they want
to do, that's fine, but I don't think that

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:09.710
that's going to always be the case, as it
stands.

00:49:09.710 --> 00:49:13.330
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: And I want to pick up on a theme
that Mike touched on, but I want to expand

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:17.710
on it based upon, you know, what are we going
to contribute as we -- as we age.

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:19.550
Aging is very new.

00:49:19.550 --> 00:49:21.850
Yes, there have been older people before.

00:49:21.850 --> 00:49:24.910
We're not the first generation of folks to
get older.

00:49:24.910 --> 00:49:29.640
We are the first generation to grow old in
these numbers, with greater expectations -- in

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:34.900
fact, the big difference is an expectations
gap -- to reinvent institutions, to build

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:40.900
new processes and to have new roles for ourselves,
so that if we continue to believe that old

00:49:40.900 --> 00:49:45.940
age is all about the numbers, and we take
today's policy apparatus, be it foreign or

00:49:45.940 --> 00:49:50.051
domestic, and we simply need to make it bigger
and better for the population, then yes, we're

00:49:50.051 --> 00:49:52.790
going to fail dismally and go bankrupt.

00:49:52.790 --> 00:49:56.870
But if we take your question as an opportunity,
which says, I've got all this human capital

00:49:56.870 --> 00:50:01.631
over age 50, 60, whatever you want to call
age; how do I invent new things to do, new

00:50:01.631 --> 00:50:07.070
ways to get it done and to stay involved,
then it's a global success, not just an individual

00:50:07.070 --> 00:50:08.070
birthday party.

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:14.480
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Well, but the other critical point
that you alluded to earlier, but did not in

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:17.720
that framework, is this lower fertility issue.

00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:24.300
I mean, if we were sitting here with an extra
30 or 40 years added to life, there would

00:50:24.300 --> 00:50:30.820
be one set of topics that one might be talking
about -- and probably wouldn't be at CFR talking

00:50:30.820 --> 00:50:37.790
about it, frankly -- and one of the core topics
would be around what do I do when I'm 94 and,

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:44.320
you know, lying there and want to -- whatever
you want to have happen to yourself --

00:50:44.320 --> 00:50:45.320
QUESTIONER: (I'd call for an ?) end.

00:50:45.320 --> 00:50:46.320
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Called to an end --

00:50:46.320 --> 00:50:47.320
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: She doesn't want to linger.

00:50:47.320 --> 00:50:48.320
(Laughter.)

00:50:48.320 --> 00:50:50.460
&gt;&gt;HODIN: -- you know, that would be one set
of discussions.

00:50:50.460 --> 00:51:00.160
Because of the low fertility that is stunningly
impactful around the world, in country after

00:51:00.160 --> 00:51:06.950
country after country, you have this profound
shift that make the institutional arrangements

00:51:06.950 --> 00:51:10.390
of the 20th century fiscally unsustainable.

00:51:10.390 --> 00:51:15.091
Which gets to the point that the gentleman
asked about, you know, Medicare and Social

00:51:15.091 --> 00:51:21.550
Security, or one can ask about the national
health scheme in the U.K., or Greece, or any

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:27.770
number of places where underlying the basic
point is that when there are fewer people

00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:33.280
working to support a larger number of people
who are traditionally retired, the numbers

00:51:33.280 --> 00:51:35.360
just don't work.

00:51:35.360 --> 00:51:44.810
So one answer, I would suggest, is that you
have people active and working, in various

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:49.640
ways of redefining work, longer and perhaps
much longer.

00:51:49.640 --> 00:51:56.340
And if we define, as the Europeans are claiming
to articulate it, a healthy and active aging,

00:51:56.340 --> 00:51:58.360
then you can actually achieve this.

00:51:58.360 --> 00:52:05.230
You know, getting from here to there is not
easy, but that's why this is the kind of topic

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:08.150
that we're attempting to address here.

00:52:08.150 --> 00:52:14.980
So you know, my answer to your question, to
go directly, you know, no, the answer is,

00:52:14.980 --> 00:52:18.760
we -- and there's some, again, in the audience
who are more familiar with this than I am

00:52:18.760 --> 00:52:24.910
and more expert -- we can fiddle with Social
Security, as we are doing, and as happens,

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:33.530
that can find at least openings for solutions
with respect to when we pay, how much we pay,

00:52:33.530 --> 00:52:36.460
how we do it.

00:52:36.460 --> 00:52:38.600
Medicare is different matter.

00:52:38.600 --> 00:52:47.130
If we -- again, you look at the five or six
-- what's now in the term of art being used

00:52:47.130 --> 00:52:52.850
is noncommunicable diseases -- you know, we
had that U.N. outcomes last year -- diabetes,

00:52:52.850 --> 00:52:56.330
cancer, Alzheimer's.

00:52:56.330 --> 00:53:01.550
You know, if those numbers continue the way
they are today -- and so this just is fiscally

00:53:01.550 --> 00:53:02.700
unsustainable.

00:53:02.700 --> 00:53:12.350
So the question is, how do we spend on finding
-- for prevention and actual solutions versus

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:13.350
care?

00:53:13.350 --> 00:53:20.030
I mean, this is a huge debate that we're -- we
must step up to.

00:53:20.030 --> 00:53:26.780
But you know, as a friend of mine looked at
a picture in the '50s of people in iron lungs,

00:53:26.780 --> 00:53:33.350
with polio, you know, how many of those could
you have warehoused, to be very blunt, you

00:53:33.350 --> 00:53:35.370
know, had we not had the Salk vaccine?

00:53:35.370 --> 00:53:37.700
Well, again, the same thing with Alzheimer's.

00:53:37.700 --> 00:53:42.280
We can care for people and be as wonderful
as we want to be, but if we don't find the

00:53:42.280 --> 00:53:47.290
way to cure it and ultimately to prevent it,
it's just not going to be manageable fiscally.

00:53:47.290 --> 00:53:53.490
So what I'm suggesting is that aging populations
and components of it are really economic policy.

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:58.680
It's not about how do we take care of 90-year-olds,
even though it will get us there.

00:53:58.680 --> 00:54:03.730
It's about how do we find economic growth
and how do we manage today's fiscal sustainability

00:54:03.730 --> 00:54:04.730
challenges?

00:54:04.730 --> 00:54:09.420
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: And part of the policy's also
about personal responsibility as well and

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:14.191
what level does one begin to treat themselves
well in their 30s so that they live well in

00:54:14.191 --> 00:54:16.420
their 40s and so on.

00:54:16.420 --> 00:54:20.070
If you go through life, as I did growing up
in Philly, believing that cheese steaks, Tastykakes

00:54:20.070 --> 00:54:24.330
and scrapple are food groups -- (laughter)
-- I'm not sure I have the right to ask any

00:54:24.330 --> 00:54:29.500
of you, when I am 67 years old and hypertensive,
diabetic and God knows what else, to, say,

00:54:29.500 --> 00:54:30.790
flip the bill.

00:54:30.790 --> 00:54:35.040
And -- but we have to have that phased discussion
on what is that new social contract.

00:54:35.040 --> 00:54:36.560
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: Yeah, I --

00:54:36.560 --> 00:54:38.610
&gt;&gt;HODIN: We have maybe one or two other questions.

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:40.220
Just to -- yes.

00:54:40.220 --> 00:54:41.580
Go ahead.

00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:43.630
It's all right.

00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:44.630
Jump in.

00:54:44.630 --> 00:54:45.630
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: OK.

00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:46.630
QUESTIONER: Thanks.

00:54:46.630 --> 00:54:48.080
Alessandro Pio from Asian Development Bank.

00:54:48.080 --> 00:54:52.260
If you compare the United States to other
countries, even with all the optimistic -- and

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:57.420
I think they are good points -- assumptions
about longer life and longer work life and

00:54:57.420 --> 00:55:00.190
so forth, one of the key differences is the
savings rate.

00:55:00.190 --> 00:55:05.360
I mean, you will still have to finance some
of these health expenditures, some of these

00:55:05.360 --> 00:55:11.200
living expenditures as a share of your income
is lower compared to your expenses.

00:55:11.200 --> 00:55:16.000
And in the United States you quibble about,
well, the savings rate is 4 percent or 5 percent,

00:55:16.000 --> 00:55:21.070
and the impact that has on the recovery, whereas
both Europe and Asian countries have saving

00:55:21.070 --> 00:55:23.690
rates in the order of 20, 30 percent.

00:55:23.690 --> 00:55:27.990
That does imply -- of course have an impact,
then, on what you can reinvest through insurance

00:55:27.990 --> 00:55:31.560
and other financial mechanisms that allow
you to pay for these things.

00:55:31.560 --> 00:55:36.160
So I think there is very basic questions about
consumption versus savings.

00:55:36.160 --> 00:55:40.380
And the consumption model that you have, you
go to a store, you cannot buy one screwdriver;

00:55:40.380 --> 00:55:41.840
you have to buy packs of six.

00:55:41.840 --> 00:55:43.740
You don't really need to do that if you only
need one.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:48.160
So I think there is some thinking about the
consumption and savings model that needs to

00:55:48.160 --> 00:55:49.400
be considered.

00:55:49.400 --> 00:55:50.980
I don't know what your views are on that.

00:55:50.980 --> 00:55:54.570
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: I think they should start manufacturing
screws that have different sizes.

00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:57.280
That would solve that.

00:55:57.280 --> 00:55:58.280
(Laughter.)

00:55:58.280 --> 00:55:59.280
But -- I'm sorry.

00:55:59.280 --> 00:56:02.520
But your point is good one, because we do
have to -- we do have to save more.

00:56:02.520 --> 00:56:08.810
And I'll tell you, policy should think about
this, because -- I'll give you a great example.

00:56:08.810 --> 00:56:13.550
We -- in the financial services market we're
using or looking a lot at behavioral finance

00:56:13.550 --> 00:56:17.980
and how people make decisions about their
savings.

00:56:17.980 --> 00:56:24.020
And there was a very successful exercise that
was put in place for auto and -- autoenrollment

00:56:24.020 --> 00:56:27.330
into your retirement plan, auto-escalation
ideas.

00:56:27.330 --> 00:56:31.360
And while I think that's a really excellent
example of some things that we can do to help

00:56:31.360 --> 00:56:37.450
increase, we also have to be really thoughtful
around the "set it and forget it" mentality,

00:56:37.450 --> 00:56:41.620
which I think is very problematic to savings.

00:56:41.620 --> 00:56:44.130
Understanding savings takes a lot of time.

00:56:44.130 --> 00:56:49.040
There is not a financial community that is
prepared or equipped really to support the

00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:50.890
education that's necessary.

00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:54.080
If you -- just think about your own work experience.

00:56:54.080 --> 00:56:58.010
And I can't speak for what it's like to work
for government, but just think about your

00:56:58.010 --> 00:57:01.830
own personal experience, how many times someone's
offered you financial education that was not

00:57:01.830 --> 00:57:04.310
personally sought out for yourself.

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:05.590
It's hard to get that.

00:57:05.590 --> 00:57:07.250
And you don't learn it in an hour.

00:57:07.250 --> 00:57:12.400
In fact, the amount of people who've told
me that the most they've ever learned about

00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:17.930
saving in their life for anything, whether
it's children's education, buying a home,

00:57:17.930 --> 00:57:20.560
retirement, they learned in their 401(k) enrollment
meeting.

00:57:20.560 --> 00:57:26.760
That's a -- that's a very problematic testimony
to our commitment to helping people understand

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:32.460
how to get there and how to save, how to balance
the choices that need to be made between saving

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:36.880
for college for their children, putting a
down payment on a home and saving for retirement.

00:57:36.880 --> 00:57:41.530
You talk to a 30-year-old, they have completely
different savings challenges than somebody

00:57:41.530 --> 00:57:44.460
who's 20 or somebody who's 40 or somebody
who's 50.

00:57:44.460 --> 00:57:49.250
And we have to help people learn how to do
that better, and maybe things like behavioral

00:57:49.250 --> 00:57:52.730
finance will help us understand how to do
that better, in a more efficient way.

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:58.640
But right now policy -- we should be thinking
about how to drive policy to help employers,

00:57:58.640 --> 00:58:04.300
which, in -- at least in the United States,
is the primary delivery system for retirement

00:58:04.300 --> 00:58:09.060
or savings products, in a more efficient way,
because right now we really don't have an

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:13.630
infrastructure to support education on a broad
basis, to help people understand how to do

00:58:13.630 --> 00:58:14.630
it.

00:58:14.630 --> 00:58:15.630
I don't know if you have --

00:58:15.630 --> 00:58:17.310
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Thank you.

00:58:17.310 --> 00:58:18.310
Hazel.

00:58:18.310 --> 00:58:22.100
QUESTIONER: Hazel Denton, Georgetown University.

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:27.810
I wanted to pick up the point about the savings
rate and also the independence that we should

00:58:27.810 --> 00:58:29.900
have, the self-care we should have.

00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:34.130
Whenever I go back home to England, I ask
my young relatives -- these are people in

00:58:34.130 --> 00:58:37.100
their 20s -- what in your pension plan?

00:58:37.100 --> 00:58:39.810
And every one of them has a good answer.

00:58:39.810 --> 00:58:45.130
Britain is moving away from these lifetime
employment situations to short-term contract

00:58:45.130 --> 00:58:46.130
work.

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:50.300
That's something I see beginning to come in
here and surely is going to be one way in

00:58:50.300 --> 00:58:53.960
which it's going to be a lot easier to hire
the older people.

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:59.890
We just have to get away from the idea that
anyone over 65 falls in the box of elderly.

00:58:59.890 --> 00:59:01.710
I think this audience will agree.

00:59:01.710 --> 00:59:02.710
(Laughter.)

00:59:02.710 --> 00:59:04.120
&gt;&gt;HODIN: Absolutely.

00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:06.910
QUESTIONER: Can I -- one more?

00:59:06.910 --> 00:59:08.790
&gt;&gt;HODIN: We have -- we have about two more
minutes.

00:59:08.790 --> 00:59:09.890
So maybe one more question.

00:59:09.890 --> 00:59:10.890
QUESTIONER: She passed it to me.

00:59:10.890 --> 00:59:12.420
I'm Arbra Johnson (sp) with Itochio (sp).

00:59:12.420 --> 00:59:17.530
And I'm hoping you can speak to the fact that
it's not just retirement that's getting later.

00:59:17.530 --> 00:59:22.470
It's having kids, buying houses, paying for
college.

00:59:22.470 --> 00:59:24.010
So how does everything have to shift?

00:59:24.010 --> 00:59:25.420
I mean, I'm 40.

00:59:25.420 --> 00:59:29.840
My youngest is 2, and I'm not atypical, at
least in the Washington region.

00:59:29.840 --> 00:59:30.840
&gt;&gt;COUGHLIN: No.

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:34.590
Over a half million women every year have
their first child after age 41 in the United

00:59:34.590 --> 00:59:35.810
States alone.

00:59:35.810 --> 00:59:38.520
In Europe they're not having children, pretty
much period.

00:59:38.520 --> 00:59:43.480
In Germany there was a headline on their newspaper
that was a -- celebrating a year ago that

00:59:43.480 --> 00:59:46.950
they had 600 more kids that year than they
did the year before.

00:59:46.950 --> 00:59:51.810
So no, things are shifting and things are
elongating -- multiple careers, cycling in

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:52.810
and out.

00:59:52.810 --> 00:59:56.240
So really longevity's rewriting all the things
that we took for granted.

00:59:56.240 --> 01:00:01.570
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: But you know, I'm -- I have a -- I
had my child at -- one child -- I'm so statistically

01:00:01.570 --> 01:00:03.530
on point with whatever you're talking about
here --

01:00:03.530 --> 01:00:04.530
MR.

01:00:04.530 --> 01:00:05.530
: Yeah.

01:00:05.530 --> 01:00:06.530
&gt;&gt;MICHEL: -- thirty-seven years old when I
had my child.

01:00:06.530 --> 01:00:11.280
And the idea of sustaining a career -- and
for many women particularly, if we're going

01:00:11.280 --> 01:00:15.140
to talk about women and retirement -- has
a lot to do with when to have that child relative

01:00:15.140 --> 01:00:16.690
to your career advancement.

01:00:16.690 --> 01:00:21.870
And for women in their 30s, those decisions
become very defining on whether you continue

01:00:21.870 --> 01:00:24.510
a career or you continue with a job.

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:28.891
And so I think there's a very important nuance
to that decision, whether it's a career or

01:00:28.891 --> 01:00:30.320
whether it's a job.

01:00:30.320 --> 01:00:35.911
And as our -- as our discussion will probably
end here shortly, it's really important, I

01:00:35.911 --> 01:00:41.090
think, to understand and to be very thoughtful
about what we want our society to be doing.

01:00:41.090 --> 01:00:45.140
Do we want to develop careers, or do we want
to develop just a job where you come in and

01:00:45.140 --> 01:00:49.570
you come out, but you have some sort of social
network beyond that that's going to help sustain

01:00:49.570 --> 01:00:50.570
you?

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:54.140
Because that's a hard thing particularly for
women to be able to do.

01:00:54.140 --> 01:01:00.380
But that saving rate should be -- the concept
of that has to start much younger for women

01:01:00.380 --> 01:01:01.410
or men, I think.

01:01:01.410 --> 01:01:04.590
&gt;&gt;HODIN: So maybe we'll conclude on two points.

01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:09.550
One is related to this, and that is when you
look at the work that is being prepared at

01:01:09.550 --> 01:01:15.390
the World Health Organization or that was
discussed in Moscow last week inside APEC

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:22.920
on the topic of aging populations -- World
Health Organization's preparation for their

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:29.540
celebration of aging at World Health Day,
which is the first week in April -- they're

01:01:29.540 --> 01:01:31.770
focused on healthy lifestyles.

01:01:31.770 --> 01:01:40.550
And it's a -- it's not about 93-year-olds
living or not; it's about healthy lifestyles.

01:01:40.550 --> 01:01:48.480
And I think, as Joe and Kelly said, what this
shift in demographics is doing to us, the

01:01:48.480 --> 01:01:55.420
longevity on the one hand, the lower fertility
on the other, is transforming how we live

01:01:55.420 --> 01:02:02.090
and what kind of institutions, both private
and public, are aligned to that, are fit for

01:02:02.090 --> 01:02:03.440
purpose.

01:02:03.440 --> 01:02:09.780
Let me conclude with a concluding comment
that Undersecretary Hormats certainly wrote

01:02:09.780 --> 01:02:15.180
yesterday and might have said if he were here:
"Working together, we can turn the longevity

01:02:15.180 --> 01:02:22.030
bequeathed us from the 20th century into a
positive driver of growth, contribution and

01:02:22.030 --> 01:02:25.160
economic activity into the 21st."

01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:30.140
Some of you may know there was a book that
was launched at Davos about a week and a half

01:02:30.140 --> 01:02:38.170
ago now -- that's two weeks ago -- titled
"Global Population Aging: Peril or Promise?"

01:02:38.170 --> 01:02:43.940
You know, I vote for the promise side, but
there's a lot of space between here and there,

01:02:43.940 --> 01:02:46.900
and I think that's what the undersecretary
was saying, and that's what we're all saying,

01:02:46.900 --> 01:02:51.680
that we have the opportunity to do this, but
it's a matter of stepping up to the policy.

01:02:51.680 --> 01:02:57.960
We were talking beforehand that one looks
for examples in history.

01:02:57.960 --> 01:03:02.120
And you don't have to go back that far to
think of some major issues that have been

01:03:02.120 --> 01:03:09.500
picked up as very profound policy challenges
with institutions to accompany them, the environment

01:03:09.500 --> 01:03:11.210
being one example.

01:03:11.210 --> 01:03:16.011
You know, pre roughly 1970, that as an issue
didn't exist, let alone -- let alone, for

01:03:16.011 --> 01:03:18.890
better and worse, having institutions around
it.

01:03:18.890 --> 01:03:24.530
Well, you know, perhaps the 21st century is
suggesting that around this aging populations,

01:03:24.530 --> 01:03:27.260
we need a similar kind of framework.

01:03:27.260 --> 01:03:28.500
So thank you all for coming.

01:03:28.500 --> 01:03:41.800
We will continue these round tables and discussions

01:03:41.800 --> 01:03:50.780
and appreciate your attention.

